## 1.1 Public-Key Fingerprint & Credential
- Identity (V2.2): `soul_hash = SHA-256(pubkey bytes)`; the hash is a public ID, not a secret; stealing it proves nothing without the private key.
- Credential: private key (Ed25519), resident only in the holder's single-device Secure Enclave/TEE; biometric approval signs inside the device.
- Authentication: challenge-response — server issues one-time nonce, device signs, server verifies signature only; nonce burnt after use.
- Server never stores or touches any private-key material; plaintext credential transport / cloud-synced private keys / hosted keys are unconstitutional.
- Key loss = identity freeze: unrecoverable, original soul_hash never re-registrable, associated memory permanently sealed (key-loss clause).
- Identity irrevocable ≠ credential unrecoverable: soul_hash (identity root) is permanently locked (Constitution Art. 6), but device credentials (Passkey/private key) can be securely replaced via social recovery (system Layer 4: guardian voting + timelock + Shamir secret sharing). Recovery replaces credentials, not the original private key; soul_hash remains unchanged, identity continuity preserved.
